  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass by or through the \nregion Sunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will persist through \ntonight before shifting off the coast of New England on \nSaturday. Tranquil conditions continue with above-normal \ntemperatures around the region. \n\nMeanwhile, broad troughing continues to reside over the \nSoutheast United States. As some upper energy arrives within \nthe sub-tropical jet today, a closed surface low will develop \nand strengthen to the east of Florida over the weekend.\n\nAlthough the 12Z suite of guidance continues to struggle as to\nhow this system will play out, there seems to be a bit of a\ntrend for the storm to take an offshore track. How far offshore\nremains to be seen. The double-barreled feature previously\nindicated by most prominently the ECMWF continues to be there on\nthe operational 12Z/20 ECMWF and even the 12Z/20 RRFS. The\n12Z/20 operational GFS is well offshore while the 12Z/20 AIGFS\nis closer with more QPF over the area. The 12Z/20 operational\nECMWF and the 12Z/20 EC-AIFS are pretty much just off the New\nJersey coast. The 12Z/20 CMC is a bit farther south compared to\nthe rest.\n\nRegardless, rain develops Sunday morning and then becomes\nwidespread Sunday afternoon and Sunday night. Rain tapers off\nMonday morning.\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has maintained has a 30 percent \nchance of Cyclone Formation within the next 48 hours, but now\nhas a 40 percent chance within the next 7 days. Regardless of \nwhether this system acquires (sub) tropical characteristics, it \nis likely that periods of heavy rain, gusty winds (mainly for \ncoastal areas), and coastal flooding may occur. The Weather \nPrediction Center maintains a Marginal risk for Excessive \nRainfall for the entire area excluding the Lehigh Valley and \nPocono region on Sunday into Sunday night. The Weather\nPrediction Center has lowered rainfall amounts by about 1/2 inch\nor so, and now a widespread 3/4 to 1 inch of rain, with a \nlocalized corridor of higher totals possible, depending on how \nthe track of the low pans out.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another low pressure system may approach the \narea around Thanksgiving.\n\nBehind the weekend system, a strong cold front will then cross \nthrough the region on Monday. This will welcome the return of \nhigh pressure which will bring much cooler (seasonable) and \ndrier conditions to the area through mid-week. \n\nGuidance then continues to indicate a highly amplified upper \nlevel pattern where there is an increasing signal for low \npressure to develop around the middle to end of next week over \nthe Southeast US. As of this writing, the NBM has 40-70 percent \nPoPs Wednesday night into Thanksgiving Day. While it is still \naround a week away, this is another disturbance worth watching \nas this may have some implications during the holiday week.",
